[연습용 문장·표현]
‘나는 그에게 우체국에 가 달라고 부탁했다.’를 ask로 영작하고, ‘He would spend hours staring at the wall.’의 would와 바꿔 쓸 수 있는 표현을 쓰시오.', 'I asked him to go to the post office. / used to (He used to spend hours staring at the wall.)', '{}'::text[], array['asked + him + to go', 'to the post office', 'would = used to (과거의 습관)'], '', '학습지3', 1, null, null, 1011, null, null),
  ('english-written-013', 'english', 'written', '가정법', null, '‘내가 부자라면 그 집을 살 수 있을 텐데.’(지금 부자가 아님)를 가정법 과거로 쓰고, If를 생략한 문장도 쓰시오.', '[연습용 문장·표현]
‘내가 부자라면 그 집을 살 수 있을 텐데.’(지금 부자가 아님)를 가정법 과거로 쓰고, If를 생략한 문장도 쓰시오.', 'If I were rich, I could buy the house. / Were I rich, I could buy the house.', '{}'::text[], array['If + I + were (be동사는 were)', 'could + 동사원형 buy', 'If 생략 시 Were I rich 도치'], '', '학습지3', 4, null, null, 1012, null, null),
  ('english-written-014', 'english', 'written', '가정법', null, '‘그는 마치 모든 것을 아는 것처럼 말한다.’와 ‘그녀는 마치 그 사고를 직접 본 것처럼 말한다.’를 as if로 영작하시오.', '[연습용 문장·표현]
‘그는 마치 모든 것을 아는 것처럼 말한다.’와 ‘그녀는 마치 그 사고를 직접 본 것처럼 말한다.’를 as if로 영작하시오.', 'He talks as if he knew everything. / She speaks as if she had seen the accident with her own eyes.', '{}'::text[], array['as if + 과거형 knew (현재의 반대)', 'as if + had seen (이전 일의 반대)', '두 문장 주절 현재시제'], '', '학습지3', 4, null, null, 1013, null, null),
  ('english-written-015', 'english', 'written', '측정의 일관성', null, '‘Following this logic, units need reliable definitions.’를 If로 시작하는 부사절 문장으로 바꾸고, 이 문장의 동사가 과거형으로 바뀌지 않는 이유를 쓰시오.', 'To harness the full potential of units of measurement, we need some degree of consistency. Subjective measures contain useful knowledge, but this can be difficult to share, based as it is on an individual’s experience. The historian of science Theodore M. Porter describes measurement as a ʻtechnology of distance’: a tool that uses shared rules to bridge differences of culture and geography, allowing for the exchange of information. Following this logic, if measurement is another sort of language, then just as with words, individual units need reliable definitions in order to communicate. In the earliest societies, these definitions might need to be shared no more widely than a single settlement, meaning that the definition can be no more complex than knowing that a unit was equal to a certain body part. As these communities grew in size, though, the inadequacies of this system would become evident. Imagine a disagreement over trade or tribute: it is a moment of conflict that demands greater consistency in measurement.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
‘Following this logic, units need reliable definitions.’를 If로 시작하는 부사절 문장으로 바꾸고, 이 문장의 동사가 과거형으로 바뀌지 않는 이유를 쓰시오.', 'If we follow this logic, units need reliable definitions. 실제로 가능한 조건을 말하는 직설법 조건문이라서 가정법처럼 시제를 과거로 바꾸지 않는다.', '{}'::text[], array['If we follow this logic', '주절 현재시제 유지', '가정법이 아닌 직설법 조건이라는 설명'], '', '학습지4', 2, null, null, 1014, null, null),
  ('english-written-016', 'english', 'written', '측정의 일관성', null, '‘It is difficult to share, based as it is on an individual’s experience.’에서 based as it is on을 as로 시작하는 절로 바꾸고, 문장 전체를 우리말로 해석하시오.', 'To harness the full potential of units of measurement, we need some degree of consistency. Subjective measures contain useful knowledge, but this can be difficult to share, based as it is on an individual’s experience. The historian of science Theodore M. Porter describes measurement as a ʻtechnology of distance’: a tool that uses shared rules to bridge differences of culture and geography, allowing for the exchange of information. Following this logic, if measurement is another sort of language, then just as with words, individual units need reliable definitions in order to communicate. In the earliest societies, these definitions might need to be shared no more widely than a single settlement, meaning that the definition can be no more complex than knowing that a unit was equal to a certain body part. As these communities grew in size, though, the inadequacies of this system would become evident. Imagine a disagreement over trade or tribute: it is a moment of conflict that demands greater consistency in measurement.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
‘It is difficult to share, based as it is on an individual’s experience.’에서 based as it is on을 as로 시작하는 절로 바꾸고, 문장 전체를 우리말로 해석하시오.', 'as it is based on an individual’s experience / 그것은 개인의 경험에 기반하기 때문에 공유하기 어렵다.', '{}'::text[], array['as it is based on', 'as = ~이기 때문에 (이유)', '개인의 경험·공유하기 어렵다 해석'], '', '학습지4', 2, null, null, 1015, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-002', 'english', 'written', 'Chichén Itzá', null, 'Made of stepped pyramids, the city is famous.

Made of ~ 부분의 문법 이름은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'Chichén Itzá was a major city of the Maya Empire from A.D. 750 to 1200. Made of stepped pyramids, temples, and other stone structures, the ancient city is now one of Mexico’s most visited tourist destinations. The largest building in Chichén Itzá is the Temple of Kukulkan ―a pyramid with 365 steps. A kind of calendar, the temple shows the change of seasons. On the spring and autumn equinoxes each year, a shadow falls on the pyramid in the shape of a snake. As the sun sets, this shadowy snake goes down the steps to eventually join a carved snake head on the pyramid’s side. The Mayans constructed the pyramids with carved stone. Amazingly, they worked without wheels or metal tools. To build a pyramid, Mayan workers created a base and added smaller and smaller levels as the structure rose. Building the pyramids required many workers. Some pyramids took hundreds of years to complete. Chichén Itzá was both an advanced city center and a religious site. Spanish records show that the Mayans made human sacrifices to a rain god here. Archaeologists have found bones, jewelry, and other objects that people wore when they were sacrificed. Experts also know that the Mayans were knowledgeable astronomers. They used the tops of the pyramids to view Venus and other planets.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
Made of stepped pyramids, the city is famous.', '분사구문', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 분사구문'], '', '학습지1', 1, null, null, 1016, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-020', 'english', 'written', 'Göbekli Tepe', null, 'one of the oldest man-made structures

man-made structures의 뜻은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is one of the oldest man-made structures on Earth. Experts believe it was built about 12,000 years ago. The structure consists of dozens of stone pillars arranged in rings. Many pillars are covered with carvings of animals. The tallest pillars are 5.5 meters in height and weigh more than 14,500 kilograms. At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system, and people did not use metal. Even wheels did not exist. Amazingly, though, the builders were able to cut, shape, and transport 16-ton stones. Archaeologists found Stone Age tools such as knives at the site. They think hundreds of workers carved and put the pillars in place. Archaeologists are still debating the purpose of Göbekli Tepe. Klaus Schmidt ― the archaeologist who originally excavated the site ― believed that Göbekli Tepe was a holy meeting place. According to his theory, the T-shaped pillars represent human beings. The pillars face the center of the circle and perhaps represent a religious ceremony. New evidence suggests that large feasts took place at the site. Archaeologists found thousands of small animal bones nearby, with stone containers large enough to hold more than 150 liters of liquid. They also found smaller pillars similar to Göbekli Tepe’s in areas over 200 kilometers away. It’s as though Göbekli Tepe were a cathedral and the other structures were local churches.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
one of the oldest man-made structures', '사람이 만든 구조물', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 사람이 만든 구조물'], '', '학습지1', 2, null, null, 1017, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-043', 'english', 'written', 'Machu Picchu', null, 'These features allowed people ____ comfortably.

빈칸에 알맞은 것은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'For hundreds of years, Machu Picchu remained hidden in the Andes Mountains of Peru. In 1911, an American explorer named Hiram Bingham introduced the ancient city to the world after local people guided him there. Today, millions of visitors travel to see its beautiful stone buildings and breathtaking mountain views. Although many facts about Machu Picchu are known, historians still debate why the city was built and why it was later abandoned. Most experts believe that Machu Picchu was built in the 1400s during the rule of the Inca emperor Pachacuti. The city was probably home to nobles, priests, and skilled workers rather than a large population. People grew crops on stone terraces that covered the mountainside. They also collected fresh water through a system of carefully planned canals and fountains. These features allowed people to live comfortably in a place that was difficult to reach. One of the most impressive features of Machu Picchu is its stonework. The Inca builders shaped huge stones so carefully that they fit together without mortar. This method made the buildings strong enough to survive many earthquakes. Even after hundreds of years of wind, rain, and earthquakes, much of the city still stands. Machu Picchu shows that the Inca people were not only skilled builders but also talented engineers who understood both nature and architecture.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
These features allowed people ____ comfortably.', 'to live', '{}'::text[], array['정답: to live'], '', '학습지1', 3, null, null, 1018, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-058', 'english', 'written', '고대 건축물 비교', null, 'They built it without wheels or metal tools.

without wheels or metal tools의 뜻은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', '[연습용 문장·표현]
They built it without wheels or metal tools.', '바퀴도 금속 도구도 없이', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 바퀴도 금속 도구도 없이'], '', '학습지1 · 1~2쪽', null, null, null, 1019, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-060', 'english', 'written', 'Bartleby: 배경과 인물', null, '법률 문서를 손으로 베껴 쓰는 직업을 뜻하는 영어 단어는? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'I am an elderly man. For the past thirty years, my work as a lawyer has brought me into contact with a particular class of men: the law-copyists, or scriveners. I have known many of them, and could tell many stories that might make you smile or weep. But I’ll leave out the biographies of all other scriveners for a few passages from the life of Bartleby, who was the strangest I ever saw or heard of. At the time of this story, my office was located on Wall Street. On one side, my windows looked out upon the white wall of the interior of a spacious sky-light shaft. On the other side, my windows commanded an unobstructed view of a lofty brick wall, black by age and everlasting shade. This view was not exactly beautiful, but it was at least quiet. I already had two scriveners: Turkey and Nippers. Turkey was a short, fat Englishman who worked wonderfully in the morning, but after his lunch, his face would turn red, and he became energetic but reckless and messy with his ink. Nippers, on the other hand, was a victim of ambition and indigestion. He was nervous and irritable in the morning, but calm and productive in the afternoon. Between them, I had a functional office, provided it was the right time of day. There was also Ginger Nut, my twelve-year-old errand boy, whose most important duty was to buy apples and ginger nuts for the scriveners. But as my business grew, I needed more help. In answer to my advertisement, a young man appeared on my office threshold one morning. He was pallidly neat, pitiably respectable, and incurably forlorn. He was Bartleby. After a brief interview, I hired him. I gave him a seat at a desk near my own, separated by a high green screen. I wanted him to be close enough for me to hand him papers, but private enough to keep his quiet nature intact.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
a person who copied legal documents by hand', 'scrivener', '{}'::text[], array['정답: scrivener'], '', '학습지2', 1, null, null, 1020, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-088', 'english', 'written', 'Bartleby: 거절과 갈등', null, 'As if famishing for something to copy, he copied eagerly.

As if의 뜻은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'At first, Bartleby did an extraordinary amount of writing. As if long famishing for something to copy, he eagerly copied my documents. He copied by sunlight and by candlelight. I would have been pleased with his hard work if he had worked cheerfully. But he wrote on silently, palely, mechanically. It was on the third day of his employment that the first crack appeared in this routine. I had a document that needed to be compared for accuracy. I called to Bartleby, asking him to step behind the screen to help me check a few lines. Without moving from his seat, Bartleby replied in a unusually gentle and firm voice: “I would prefer not to.” I sat in stunned silence. I thought my ears had deceived me. I repeated my request, but the response was exactly the same. “I would prefer not to.” I looked at him. He was perfectly calm. There was no sign of anger, impatience, or rudeness in his face. Had he seemed angry or rude, I would have fired him immediately. But his calmness fascinated and paralyzed me. I could not make myself argue with him. As days passed, the situation grew more bizarre. One morning, I asked him to go to the post office; he replied that he would prefer not to. I asked him to call the other clerks to help with a task; he preferred not to. Eventually, I discovered that he had stopped writing altogether. He would spend hours standing behind his screen, staring out the window at the dead brick wall. I tried to talk to him and persuade him. “Bartleby,” I said, “why will you not write? Why will you not do anything?” “I would prefer not to,” he replied, and nothing more.

My emotions toward him were a strange mixture. Sometimes I felt a deep, melancholy pity. I realized that Bartleby was alone in the world — a lonely soul in the middle of the busy, commercial desert of Wall Street. I discovered that he never ate a full dinner; he lived almost entirely on ginger nuts. Even more shockingly, I found out one Sunday morning that he was actually living in the office. He slept on the sofa, washed his face in a small basin, and spent his weekends in the total silence of the empty business district. However, pity soon turned to a sense of dread. His presence began to affect the atmosphere of the office. I found myself, and even Turkey and Nippers, unconsciously using the word ‘prefer’ in our daily speech. His passive resistance was like a quiet plague. I felt that my authority was slowly fading before a man who did nothing, said nothing, and wanted nothing. When I finally told him he must leave, he simply preferred not to. I offered him money; he let it lie on the table. I realized that as long as he remained in my office, he was my responsibility. But the pressure from my professional friends and clients became too much. They wondered why I kept such a ‘useless fixture’ in my office. Desperate to be rid of him, and unable to throw this pale, helpless man into the street, I decided on an extreme measure: I moved my entire office to a new location, leaving Bartleby behind in the empty rooms. But the story did not end there. A few days later, the new tenant of my old office came to me in a state of great excitement. Bartleby was still there. He sat on the stairs by day and slept in the entry hall by night. Everyone was disturbed. To avoid a public scandal, I went back and tried to talk to him one last time. “Bartleby," I said, “would you like to work as a store clerk?” “I would prefer not to. I am not particular,” he said. “Would you like a job traveling with someone through Europe?” “Not at all. I would prefer not to.”

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
As if famishing for something to copy, he copied eagerly.', '마치 ~인 것처럼', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 마치 ~인 것처럼'], '', '학습지3', 1, null, null, 1021, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-120', 'english', 'written', 'Bartleby: 결말과 상징', null, 'Bartleby was taken to the Tombs.

the Tombs는 무엇일까? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'I gave up. The new tenants eventually called the police, and Bartleby was taken to the Tombs as a homeless person. When I visited him in prison, he was standing alone in a quiet courtyard, staring at a high wall. “I know where I am,” he said to me, refusing to look at me. “I have nothing to say to you.” I spoke to the prison cook, paying him to ensure Bartleby had good food. But when the cook offered him dinner, Bartleby turned his face toward the wall and said, "I prefer not to eat today." He had decided to stop eating. A few days later, I found him lying in the yard, his head resting against the stones, his eyes open but vacant. He was dead. Some months later, I heard a rumor that before coming to me, Bartleby had been a subordinate clerk in the Dead Letter Office at Washington. He had spent years sorting letters that were sent to people who were already dead, or letters that contained rings and money that would never reach their destination. These letters, intended for life, were destined for the flames. When I heard this, I could finally understand his silence and his ‘dead-wall reveries.’ Surrounded by the failed hopes and dead messages of humanity, his soul had simply given up. He had seen too much of the end of things. Ah, Bartleby! Ah, humanity!

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
Bartleby was taken to the Tombs.', '뉴욕 감옥의 별명', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 뉴욕 감옥의 별명'], '', '학습지3', 3, null, null, 1022, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-128', 'english', 'written', '가정법과 시제', null, 'If I ___ rich, I would travel the world.

빈칸에 알맞은 것은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', '[연습용 문장·표현]
If I ___ rich, I would travel the world.', 'were', '{}'::text[], array['정답: were'], '', '학습지3', 4, null, null, 1023, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-140', 'english', 'written', '예술과 모방', null, 'tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parrhasios

여기서 that이 가리키는 것은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'The ancient Greeks felt that the visual artist’s goal was to copy visual experience. This approach appears in the realism of ancient Greek sculpture and pottery. We must sadly note that, due to the action of time and weather, no paintings from ancient Greek artists exist today. We can only surmise their quality based on tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parhassios, the obvious skill in ancient Greek sculpture, and in drawings that survive on ancient Greek pottery. This definition of art as copying reality has a problem, though. Jackson Pollock, a leader in the New York School of the 1950’s, intentionally did not copy existing objects in his art. While painting these works, Pollock and his fellow artists would consciously avoid making marks or passages that resembled recognizable objects. They succeeded at making artwork that did not copy anything, thus demonstrating that the ancient Greek view of art as mimesis ― simple copying ― does not sufficiently define art.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
tales such as that of Zeuxis and Parrhasios', 'tale', '{}'::text[], array['정답: tale'], '', '학습지4', 1, null, null, 1024, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-153', 'english', 'written', '측정의 일관성', null, 'As these communities grew in size, the system failed.

grew in size의 뜻은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'To harness the full potential of units of measurement, we need some degree of consistency. Subjective measures contain useful knowledge, but this can be difficult to share, based as it is on an individual’s experience. The historian of science Theodore M. Porter describes measurement as a ʻtechnology of distance’: a tool that uses shared rules to bridge differences of culture and geography, allowing for the exchange of information. Following this logic, if measurement is another sort of language, then just as with words, individual units need reliable definitions in order to communicate. In the earliest societies, these definitions might need to be shared no more widely than a single settlement, meaning that the definition can be no more complex than knowing that a unit was equal to a certain body part. As these communities grew in size, though, the inadequacies of this system would become evident. Imagine a disagreement over trade or tribute: it is a moment of conflict that demands greater consistency in measurement.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
As these communities grew in size, the system failed.', '규모가 커졌다', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 규모가 커졌다'], '', '학습지4', 2, null, null, 1025, null, null),
  ('practice-short-english-168', 'english', 'written', '공통 문법·독해', null, 'Even though it was old, the pillar still stood.

Even though의 뜻은? (답을 하나만 쓰세요.)', 'Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is one of the oldest man-made structures on Earth. Experts believe it was built about 12,000 years ago. The structure consists of dozens of stone pillars arranged in rings. Many pillars are covered with carvings of animals. The tallest pillars are 5.5 meters in height and weigh more than 14,500 kilograms. At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system, and people did not use metal. Even wheels did not exist. Amazingly, though, the builders were able to cut, shape, and transport 16-ton stones. Archaeologists found Stone Age tools such as knives at the site. They think hundreds of workers carved and put the pillars in place. Archaeologists are still debating the purpose of Göbekli Tepe. Klaus Schmidt ― the archaeologist who originally excavated the site ― believed that Göbekli Tepe was a holy meeting place. According to his theory, the T-shaped pillars represent human beings. The pillars face the center of the circle and perhaps represent a religious ceremony. New evidence suggests that large feasts took place at the site. Archaeologists found thousands of small animal bones nearby, with stone containers large enough to hold more than 150 liters of liquid. They also found smaller pillars similar to Göbekli Tepe’s in areas over 200 kilometers away. It’s as though Göbekli Tepe were a cathedral and the other structures were local churches.

[연습용 문장·표현]
Even though it was old, the pillar still stood.', '비록 ~이지만', '{}'::text[], array['정답: 비록 ~이지만'], '', '학습지1', 2, null, null, 1026, null, null),
  ('practice-written-english-001', 'english', 'written', 'Chichén Itzá', null, 'Made of stone, the city still stands.

문장 앞에 생략된 접속사로 알맞은 것은? 답과 그 이유를 연결하여 서술하시오.', 'Chichén Itzá was a major city of the Maya Empire from A.D. 750 to 1200. Made of stepped pyramids, temples, and other stone structures, the ancient city is now one of Mexico’s most visited tourist destinations. The largest building in Chichén Itzá is the Temple of Kukulkan ―a pyramid with 365 steps. A kind of calendar, the temple shows the change of seasons. On the spring and autumn equinoxes each year, a shadow falls on the pyramid in the shape of a snake. As the sun sets, this shadowy snake goes down the steps to eventually join a carved snake head on the pyramid’s side. The Mayans constructed the pyramids with carved stone. Amazingly, they worked without wheels or metal tools. To build a pyramid, Mayan workers created a base and added smaller and smaller levels as the structure rose. Building the pyramids required many workers. Some pyramids took hundreds of years to complete. Chichén Itzá was both an advanced city center and a religious site. Spanish records show that the Mayans made human sacrifices to a rain god here. Archaeologists have found bones, jewelry, and other objects that people wore when they were sacrificed. Experts also know that the Mayans were knowledgeable astronomers. They used the tops of the pyramids to view Venus and other planets.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
Made of stone, the city still stands.', 'Because. Made of stone은 이유를 나타내는 분사구문이다. Because(As) it is made of stone에서 접속사·주어·be동사가 생략됐다.', '{}'::text[], array['결론: Because', '근거: Made of stone은 이유를 나타내는 분사구문이다. Because(As) it is made of stone에서 접속사·주어·be동사가 생략됐다.'], '', '학습지1', 1, null, null, 1027, null, null),
  ('practice-written-english-020', 'english', 'written', 'Göbekli Tepe', null, 'one of the oldest man-made structures

man-made structures의 뜻은? 답과 그 이유를 연결하여 서술하시오.', 'Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is one of the oldest man-made structures on Earth. Experts believe it was built about 12,000 years ago. The structure consists of dozens of stone pillars arranged in rings. Many pillars are covered with carvings of animals. The tallest pillars are 5.5 meters in height and weigh more than 14,500 kilograms. At the time that Göbekli Tepe was built, there was no writing system, and people did not use metal. Even wheels did not exist. Amazingly, though, the builders were able to cut, shape, and transport 16-ton stones. Archaeologists found Stone Age tools such as knives at the site. They think hundreds of workers carved and put the pillars in place. Archaeologists are still debating the purpose of Göbekli Tepe. Klaus Schmidt ― the archaeologist who originally excavated the site ― believed that Göbekli Tepe was a holy meeting place. According to his theory, the T-shaped pillars represent human beings. The pillars face the center of the circle and perhaps represent a religious ceremony. New evidence suggests that large feasts took place at the site. Archaeologists found thousands of small animal bones nearby, with stone containers large enough to hold more than 150 liters of liquid. They also found smaller pillars similar to Göbekli Tepe’s in areas over 200 kilometers away. It’s as though Göbekli Tepe were a cathedral and the other structures were local churches.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
one of the oldest man-made structures', '사람이 만든 구조물. man-made는 "사람이 만든, 인공의"다. 남자(man)만을 뜻하지 않는다.', '{}'::text[], array['결론: 사람이 만든 구조물', '근거: man-made는 "사람이 만든, 인공의"다. 남자(man)만을 뜻하지 않는다.'], '', '학습지1', 2, null, null, 1028, null, null),
  ('practice-written-english-043', 'english', 'written', 'Machu Picchu', null, 'These features allowed people ____ comfortably.

빈칸에 알맞은 것은? 답과 그 이유를 연결하여 서술하시오.', 'For hundreds of years, Machu Picchu remained hidden in the Andes Mountains of Peru. In 1911, an American explorer named Hiram Bingham introduced the ancient city to the world after local people guided him there. Today, millions of visitors travel to see its beautiful stone buildings and breathtaking mountain views. Although many facts about Machu Picchu are known, historians still debate why the city was built and why it was later abandoned. Most experts believe that Machu Picchu was built in the 1400s during the rule of the Inca emperor Pachacuti. The city was probably home to nobles, priests, and skilled workers rather than a large population. People grew crops on stone terraces that covered the mountainside. They also collected fresh water through a system of carefully planned canals and fountains. These features allowed people to live comfortably in a place that was difficult to reach. One of the most impressive features of Machu Picchu is its stonework. The Inca builders shaped huge stones so carefully that they fit together without mortar. This method made the buildings strong enough to survive many earthquakes. Even after hundreds of years of wind, rain, and earthquakes, much of the city still stands. Machu Picchu shows that the Inca people were not only skilled builders but also talented engineers who understood both nature and architecture.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
These features allowed people ____ comfortably.', 'to live. allow + 목적어 + to V는 "~가 …하게 해 주다"다. 목적격 보어 자리에 to부정사가 온다.', '{}'::text[], array['결론: to live', '근거: allow + 목적어 + to V는 "~가 …하게 해 주다"다. 목적격 보어 자리에 to부정사가 온다.'], '', '학습지1', 3, null, null, 1029, null, null),
  ('practice-written-english-058', 'english', 'written', '고대 건축물 비교', null, 'They built it without wheels or metal tools.

without wheels or metal tools의 뜻은? 답과 그 이유를 연결하여 서술하시오.', '[연습용 문장·표현]
They built it without wheels or metal tools.', '바퀴도 금속 도구도 없이. without A or B = A도 B도 없이. 마야인과 괴베클리 테페의 건설자들은 바퀴도 금속 도구도 없이 거대한 돌을 다뤘다.', '{}'::text[], array['결론: 바퀴도 금속 도구도 없이', '근거: without A or B = A도 B도 없이. 마야인과 괴베클리 테페의 건설자들은 바퀴도 금속 도구도 없이 거대한 돌을 다뤘다.'], '', '학습지1 · 1~2쪽', null, null, null, 1030, null, null),
  ('practice-written-english-060', 'english', 'written', 'Bartleby: 배경과 인물', null, 'a person who copied legal documents by hand

이런 일을 한 사람을 뜻하는 말은? 답과 그 이유를 연결하여 서술하시오.', 'I am an elderly man. For the past thirty years, my work as a lawyer has brought me into contact with a particular class of men: the law-copyists, or scriveners. I have known many of them, and could tell many stories that might make you smile or weep. But I’ll leave out the biographies of all other scriveners for a few passages from the life of Bartleby, who was the strangest I ever saw or heard of. At the time of this story, my office was located on Wall Street. On one side, my windows looked out upon the white wall of the interior of a spacious sky-light shaft. On the other side, my windows commanded an unobstructed view of a lofty brick wall, black by age and everlasting shade. This view was not exactly beautiful, but it was at least quiet. I already had two scriveners: Turkey and Nippers. Turkey was a short, fat Englishman who worked wonderfully in the morning, but after his lunch, his face would turn red, and he became energetic but reckless and messy with his ink. Nippers, on the other hand, was a victim of ambition and indigestion. He was nervous and irritable in the morning, but calm and productive in the afternoon. Between them, I had a functional office, provided it was the right time of day. There was also Ginger Nut, my twelve-year-old errand boy, whose most important duty was to buy apples and ginger nuts for the scriveners. But as my business grew, I needed more help. In answer to my advertisement, a young man appeared on my office threshold one morning. He was pallidly neat, pitiably respectable, and incurably forlorn. He was Bartleby. After a brief interview, I hired him. I gave him a seat at a desk near my own, separated by a high green screen. I wanted him to be close enough for me to hand him papers, but private enough to keep his quiet nature intact.

[문제의 대상 문장·표현]
a person who copied legal documents by hand', 'scrivener (필경사). scrivener(필경사)는 법률 문서와 계약서를 손으로 베껴 쓰던 사람이다. 기계 부품처럼 대체 가능한 존재로 취급됐다.', '{}'::text[], array['결론: scrivener (필경사)', '근거: scrivener(필경사)는 법률 문서와 계약서를 손으로 베껴 쓰던 사람이다. 기계 부품처럼 대체 가능한 존재로 취급됐다.'], '', '학습지2', 1, null, null, 1031, null, null),
